This time, all three main characters are directly under the player's control. In addition to them, both Zeza and Elric, provided they survived the last mission, are on the field as the mission begins, along with that one saurian skirmisher who talked a bit during the opening dialogue. In addition Silas can now recruit Ghouls n' Ghosts! (Wide grin)\par
The opponent is fairly aggressive, as orcs often are, recruiting many grunts and archers and attacking directly through the northern passage. Whatever sentries are occupying the ruins around his camp, however, mostly stay where they are. All cities on the map belong to the enemy by default. But he mostly ignores the eastern front, which is a great advantage for Xel and his saurians.\par
\par
Mission objective: Kill the enemy commander. Mission will fail if Cheska, Silan, or Xel ever lose their lives. There is no time limit and the weather is clean.\par
This information will not be provided from this mission forward: it will simply all be automatically assumed to be true, and nothing is said about it unless exceptions are made.\par
\par
Turn 2 opens with the following little piece of dialogue:\par
Silan: "You know, I have to say I am positively surprised by your attitude. Most people simply try to kill me on sight, no matter what the circumstances might be!"\par
Cheska: "I am still considering it. Don't push me."\par
\par
Some more dialogue will ensue as soon as the player captures any one of the villages in the immediate vicinity of the map:\par
Peasant (that shows up from inside): "No! Noo! Stay away from me, monster, stay away from-"\par
Silan: "Hush! Relax, friend! I am not going to harm you.\par
Peasant: "...Oh...? You don't intend to stab me at night or anything like that...?"\par
Silan: "Rest assured, that is not my intention. My troops may look frightening, but all we are looking to do here is to liberate this area from orcs."\par
Peasant: "Oh, well... I suppose that's okay, then. It's about time for that, you know, we've been under their occupation for a week now! It has not been fun; many of us have died already, and the rest are utterly miserable."\par
Cheska: "Well, we are here to change that."\par
The peasant then joins your party. He won't do much by himself, but maybe you can keep him alive long enough to upgrade him to something stronger.\par
\par
On turn 3, the circumstances change a bit, when the eastern swamp castle is suddenly occupied by drakes. All swamp and water villages (minus the westernmost one, the one very close to your starting location) immediately go to their possession. They are led by a drake warrior called Gak, and they are considered an enemy by both the player and the orcs.\par
\par
Xel reacts strongly upon seeing them:\par
Xel: "Oh, no. No. No!"\par
Gak: "Oh, hello there! You must be the one we're looking for."\par
Cheska: "Xel? Do you know them?"\par
Gak: "The name's Gak, human lady, and I seek to bring this little fugitive back home, dead or alive. They're offering quite a bit of money for his head!"\par
Xel: "They... do? Why? I am not that important!"\par
Gak: "Not a clue. I think they were explaining something about the whole thing, but to be honest, I didn't much listen. I only want the money."\par
Xel: "Oh..." \par
He looks obviously relieved - whether it's because these people are just mercenaries instead of a real force of justice, or because they know not of his past, is unknown at the present.\par
Cheska: "Are you sure you can handle this guy? I could help you."\par
Gak: "Do not interfere! I have no business with you, and you have no business with this cretin!"\par
Cheska: "I might have."\par
Xel: "No. This is my fight. And your troops don't fare well at the swamp anyway."\par
...\par
Xel: "But... Cheska?"\par
Cheska: "Yes?"\par
Xel: "I really appreciate the notion. Thanks."\par
Cheska: "Think nothing of it."\par
\par
Despite having just invaded a bunch of cities from his immediate vicinity, Gak doesn't have all that much in terms of money, and will not do very much recruitment. He and his troops will focus primarily upon saurians, but are willing to attack just about anything else if there's none of them around.\par
\par
Updated mission objective: Kill both enemy commanders.\par
\par
If/when Xel makes contact with the enemy drakes:\par
Gak: "Oho, little lizard comes to the fight himself! Have you done running away?"\par
Xel: "I will not run away from filth like you!"\par
\par
And when Gak finally falls, he has this to say:\par
Xel: "You were killed for the sake of money. Was it really worth it?"\par
Gak: "Dying... is never worth... it..."\par
Silan: "I beg to differ."\par
Cheska: "I'm sure you would."\par
Silan: "I did not mean it like that! For your information, I have no intention of coming back to this world when I finally snuff it. I simply meant that yes, there are indeed causes worth dying for."\par
Cheska: "I suppose there are."\par
\par
---\par
On victory:\par
Cheska: "Both orcs and drakes have been routed. The victory is ours!"\par
Silan: "So..."\par
Cheska: "Yes? What?"\par
Silan: "Have I earned your trust yet?"\par
Cheska: "Hmm... perhaps not quite. But I think I will trust you enough to not put you back in chains."\par
Silan: "Many thanks! I know how hard it is for a loyal Wesnothian human to trust a necromancer, and rest assured, I have no intention to betray that trust."\par
Cheska: "See that you don't."\par
Silan: "Furthermore, are you thinking what I am thinking? About this place?"\par
Cheska: "If what you are thinking is that it might make a good base of operations to our war against orcs, then yes, we are on the same carriage of thought."\par
Silan: "Oh, indeed! Perhaps we might also recruit a few peasants? They do not seem to be as frightened of me here as they would be in the heart of the nation."\par
Cheska: "Perhaps so. Although I do think they could use a bit of that fear. It would keep them wary."\par
\par
Xel: "Um, Cheska? Can we talk?"\par
Cheska: "Certainly. What's in your mind?"\par
Xel: "I... While I really appreciate what you did just now, with your offer of help... I really don't think it was all that appropriate."\par
Cheska: "Why is that?"\par
Xel: "We have only known each other for a few days. It is such a little time. You know nothing of me, and even less of the circumstances between me and the rest of my kind. I do not deserve your help."\par
Silan: "Oh, don't be such a fool. Cheska offered her aid because she wanted to help you, that's all. None of us would wish to see a little guy like you getting bullied by big mean lizards such as that Gak fellow. Mighty good job you did with him, by the way."\par
Xel: "...Thank you."\par
\par
***********************************\par
\par
Chapter Three: The Valley of Early Winter\par
\par
And so it came to be that this city, and especially the abandoned fortress, became their base of operations, from which they ran several counter-raids against the orcish forces, helped defending some more nearby cities, and spent their free time repairing the fortress and training recruited citizens. Weeks passed.\par
\par
Cheska sent further messages to the inner country, detailing her struggle against the orcs, hearing news about the battle elsewhere as well as eventually gaining some reinforcements. These, unfortunately, were not as accustomed to the presence of a necromancer, forcing Silan to relocate his own business lest he would bring unwanted attention towards him and his companions. As Silan left north to deal with some disturbance among elves living there, Cheska and Xel sailed east, upriver, in order to investigate rumours of undead activities thereat.\par
\par
The first snowflakes were falling, as fall was bringing way to winter...\par
\par
---\par
[spoiler=The map in question, hidden for your viewing pleasure][img]http://img22.imageshack.us/img22/9898/kentt3.jpg[/img][/spoiler]\par
(Imagine a thick fog covering this whole map and obstructing your view. [i]Then[/i] imagine you could see through the fog, that the whole place is pretty full of all kinds of undead. Gets pretty confusing, huh?)\par
\par
This mission begins with a ship arriving from the west, unloading Cheska, Xel, and what named characters still are alive within your party. They set a base camp upon the place shown on the map.\par
Cheska: "Now this is peculiar. It's... not supposed to be quite this cold yet, at the time of the year."\par
Xel: "At the time of the year? Are you saying... for your country, at some point of the year, this kind of weather is [i]normal?[/i]\par
Cheska: "Actually, I'm afraid it will get even worse."\par
Xel: "...I think me and my people might need some warmer clothing."\par
\par
Xel: "Still, you mentioned such cold weather was not normal this early."\par
Cheska: "Yes."\par
Xel: "Any ideas?"\par
Cheska: "When enough mages with sufficient power and knowledge get together and make some deliberate attempt, they are capable of bending, slightly, the weather conditions around them. Usually that would mean necromancers creating winter: it has been known to happen, once or twice in the history of Wesnoth."\par
Xel: "...Or it might simply be of abnormal weather."\par
Cheska: "I admire your optimism."\par
Xel: "I'm simply observing all possibilities of the situation."\par
\par
---\par
Thanks to the reinforcements Cheska has received in the past couple weeks, she is now capable of recruiting cavalrymen and mages. Xel, meanwhile, managed to hire services of whatever was left of Gak's mercenary group, allowing him to recruit drake fighters and gliders.\par
The enemy is, this time around, the undead. They start out in a fairly passive manner, the bunch they've got hidden within the mist (skeletons and skeleton archers, and a couple wraiths and shadows) simply waiting for the enemy to arrive - but once the player makes contact, they become much more aggressive. They start the scenario with all the villages already in their control, allowing them to belch out even more undead in a reasonably fast pace.\par
\par
Mission objective, at first, is to investigate the valley that is the battleground in question. As mentioned, fog is present, obstructing the view for most of the field. As soon as it does become clear there is undead there (i.e. when one is seen), dialogue follows:\par
Xel: "All right, so the valley [i]is[/i] infested with living dead. I suppose it was rather obvious, in hindsight."\par
Cheska: "Indeed. Our duty is now clear: we must clear the path of any trace of undead or necromancer filth, then press on forwards."\par
Xel: "Roger that. I only wish it was warmer..."\par
\par
Now, see that one house surrounded by woods, a little bit to the left from the very center of the map? This house is in fact the only one that has not fallen into the hands of the undead, and they will avoid it no matter what. Investigating the matter will trigger this piece of dialogue:\par
White mage: "Who is it out there now? More of the accursed undead?"\par
Cheska: "No, ma'am. We are quite alive and well. My name is Cheska Kuhral, and this is my associate, Xel."\par
Xel: "Hello."\par
Cheska: "We are here, initially, to investigate some strange rumours concerning the place as of late: once those rumours turned out to be completely true, our task turned into that of purging the valley of necromancers."\par
White mage: "And I am Dorothy Salminen, the resident retired white mage of the area. It is a pleasure to make acquaintance with you all, and I wish you best of luck. I might have dealt with the problem myself, if I was a few decades younger, but with my age, unfortunately, I am barely enough to defend my own cottage."\par
Xel: "Impressive work, even so, from an old woman such as you. No offense or anything."\par
Dorothy: "None taken."\par
Cheska: "Well, the issue was that you were all alone, was it not? Even if you were no match to the legions of the undead alone, I believe you would still be significant aid to us. Would you consider lending us your aid?"\par
Dorothy: "It would be a pleasure."\par
Cheska: "Much appreciated."\par
\par
If Dorothy is ever attacked: "Oh, so you are evil enough to attack a frail, helpless old woman? Well I happen to have a few surprises in store, reserved for exactly the rotten cretins of your kind!"\par
\par
If she ever dies: "I... I can see the light. You shall not be following me."\par
\par
---\par
On victory:\par
Xel: "Ah-choo! (Sneeze) Oh, wonderful."\par
Cheska: "Are you sure you will be all right?"\par
Xel: "Just a bit of cold I caught. I'll be fine."\par
Cheska: "No, really... it might develop into something nasty. You should probably head back to camp and get some rest."\par
Xel: "I told you, I'll be fi- [i]and no cuddling![/i] (Cough)"\par
Cheska: "...What? I was not going to cuddle you."\par
Xel: "I saw it in your eyes. You think I'm a cute little lizard who has- (Sneeze) who has gotten ill and requires your care and affection. Well let me tell you I'm not! I am a warrior, and such thoughts are demeaning to my status!"\par
Cheska: "Forgive me. I did not intend you to think that way of me. I was merely trying to help."\par
Xel: "..."\par
Cheska: "Would you at least accept this blanket, to help keep you warm? No unfortunate implications attached."\par
Xel: "...(Muttering) I suppose."\par
\par
The following dialogue will only occur if Dorothy the white mage is still alive:\par
Xel: "...Why are you looking at me like that? (Sniff)"\par
Dorothy: "Oh, do pardon my manners. It is simply that I do not often see saurians in these parts, especially at winter, [i]especially[/i] accompanying a human army."\par
Xel: "You do see a lot of weird things in this world, right?"\par
Dorothy: "Indeed."\par
Xel: "Say, could you possibl- (hack) possibly be capable of doing something for this condition I'm in?"\par
Dorothy: "I am afraid not. A common cold is simply one of those nuisances every human - and saurian - must occasionally endure. I can perhaps protect you from developing anything worse, or make some tea, but that is all the help you can expect from me."\par
Xel: "...Well, bummer."\par
\par
Cheska: "Ma'am, if you intend to accompany me on my travels out of your home, then I feel there is something you should know, about a companion of mine..."\par
Dorothy: "Indeed? Is this one as curious as your lizard friend?"\par
Cheska: "He is a necromancer."\par
Dorothy: "...I beg your pardon?"\par
Xel: "Just like you heard (sniffle). He isn't that bad of a guy, all things considered - he only uses orc bodies and- (hack) and doesn't seem to have much interest in taking over the country or anything."\par
Cheska: "I am still somewhat cautious of him myself, but I would not wish him to be smited by your righteous fury unless he deserved it. He has been a great help."\par
Dorothy: "Hmm. You travel with a saurian and what seems to be a necromancer of somewhat benevolent nature? I am most definitely going to continue my travels with you! This sounds interesting!"\par
Cheska: "Is it just me, or did you just get younger?"\par
Dorothy: "'Tis my desire for adventure that is renewing me!"\par
\par
Cheska: "..."\par
Cheska: "...No, I do not believe this was the source of the cold - merely a forward outpost."\par
Dorothy: "It may have done its part in the freezing process, however, if only small."\par
(If Dorothy has died, the above comment goes to Xel instead.)\par
Cheska: "Indeed, but to discover the main culprit, we must press on further east. Onward!"\par
\par
***********************************\par
\par
- All the heroes ever talk about in other campaigns is war stuff and all kinds of things relevant to it. There's never any personal issues - such as one of them catching a cold. I have to say it's one of the reasons I love the dialogue in Baldur's Gate so much.\par
(By the way, cold weather doesn't actually increase the risk of getting ill, but I decided to use it nonetheless for the sake of storytelling.)\par
- So, of course Cheska's messages have included information about a necromancer she caught, therefore whoever sent her reinforcements must have had knowledge about him: thus, why didn't they ever question his total absence? Maybe she told them she executed him herself.\par
\par
***********************************\par
\par
Chapter Four: The Day Autumn Came Back\par
\par
They did not need to travel for long before they found the headquarters of the necromancers. Indeed, even with their power, they could not have spread their tainted winter too far.\par
\par
---\par
[spoiler=This was supposed to be big and high-quality, but MSPaint failed me. I guess][img]http://img42.imageshack.us/img42/659/mission4.png[/img][/spoiler]\par
(The fog is present once again, preventing you to see the details. But if you could see them, you'd see a bunch of undead wandering about:  ghosts, wraiths, and shadows, mainly, very little made of tangible matter.)\par
\par
Cheska: "This looks like an old dwarven mining camp."\par
Dorothy (assuming she lives): "You would be correct. I used to come here many times when I was a young girl: the dwarves used to be so friendly, still here at the time, mining their precious metals from the ground. They are long gone now, of course."\par
Xel: "Something else must have (sniffle) taken over. (Sneeze)"\par
Cheska: "And we are here to pry it out."\par
\par
---\par
The undead already on the field mostly walk around pretty randomly, but focus upon the player if they ever see them.\par
One of the enemy generals, stationed on the bottom base, is a Lich: it will be enough to defeat him to win the scenario. He plays defensively, sticks in his base, occasionally recruits something or two for protection, letting his necromancer ally to do most of the menial work. The Lich's minions are all of second level, but thankfully they are not meny; the living necromancer's weaker undead bunch are far more numerous and aggressive here.\par
Like with the last map, fog obscures vision here.\par
\par
(On another note, the cold Xel is having does not affect his battle prowess at all. It does, however, make him feel absolutely miserable. Poor guy.)\par
\par
Once the Lich finally comes to sight, plot happens:\par
Lich: "Ssss! Living ones! What do you want of me?!"\par
Cheska: "We seek answers, foul Lich! What are you doing here? For what purpose do you freeze the land?"\par
Lich: "Freeze the land? I do not do such a thing."\par
Cheska: "Wait. ...What? You do not?"\par
Lich: "Indeed I do not. Your hearing is truly impeccable. The decreased temperature is simply a side effect for the project I am running here."\par
Xel: "Side effect...? For what?"\par
Lich: "I do not intend to tell you this. Prepare to meet your end, and beyond!"\par
\par
When the Lich finally goes down, he has this to say: "NO! I was... so close...!"\par
\par
---\par
On victory:\par
Cheska: "So close to what, I wonder?"\par
Xel: "Look! (Sniffle) They have been excavating over here. And... Brrr! It suddenly got so cold!"\par
Dorothy (or Cheska if Dorothy's been killed): "It would seem as if this hole is the source of the cold. The Lich had been digging something up."\par
A random soldier in your command: "Ma'am! It is impossible for us to investigate further due to extreme cold!"\par
Cheska: "The undead can withstand freezing temperatures far better than anything living. I suspect that is what allowed them to do this in the first place."\par
Xel: "If only Silan was here right now. He could- (hack) check that place out with those minions of his."\par
Cheska: "This is correct. We will ask him to delve further into this once we meet again. But for now, let us seal this hole."\par
\par
Xel: "Am I- (sneeze) Is my presence required anymore...?"\par
Cheska: "No. You can go back to the camp now."\par
Xel: "That is what I intended. I will bury myself under the bedsheets and (cough) not come out until spring!"\par
\par
If Dorothy is alive, the following is added:\par
Dorothy: "I think he is rather cute, in his own special way. Would you agree?"\par
Cheska: "I... I suppose."\par
Xel: "I am [i]not[/i] cute! Children are cute. Pets are cute. Like... hamsters, and other adorable fuzzy little things. But I am not!"\par
\par
***********************************\par
\par
- This chapter was a short one. I suppose they just don't have so much stuff to talk about with Silan away.\par
- Which of course inevitably leads into an even shorter chapter: as you might have guessed, we're going to check out what Silan's been doing for this time, and he is all alone, with only the dead to talk to.\par
Actually, let's do that right away:\par
\par
Chapter 5:\par
Silan: "...I'm bored."\par
The end\par
\par
There, wasn't that just epic? Brace for chapter 6 which will hopefully be up tomorrow! It sure promises to be an epic one!\par
- By the way... this is supposed to be an isolated, empty area. Even dwarves left a long time ago. So why are there so damn many villages around here? Why are there always villages everywhere you go in Wesnoth, even at the edge of the earth where no man has ever gone before? This really bugs me.\par
- I'm also slightly wary about using a Lich in this mission. In my experience, they're supposed to be really rare and usually the final bosses of campaigns. I'm trying to conserve my Liches in the future.\par
\par
