\par
Chapter Five: Phantoms in the Forest\par
\par
The southeastern areas of Wesnoth were not the only ones with a problem with orcs. Having heard rumours of them assaulting the eastern elven towns, and after being forced to depart from his companions for the time being, Silan had decided to investigate upon the matter. Unfortunately, elves were no less hostile towards necromancy than humans were...\par
\par
---\par
[spoiler][img]http://img34.imageshack.us/img34/8805/mission5.png[/img][/spoiler\par
(The fog surrounds the area, hiding from your vision such things as elven and orcish combatants locked in a deadly battle. There are far more orcs around, though.)\par
\par
For obvious reasons, Silan wishes to remain very much hidden during this battle. Therefore, as soon as he arrives on the battlefield, he summons a thick fog to cover his intentions from the elves as well as orcs.\par
Silan: "Sometimes, justice is as dark as the evil it is fighting, and must remain hidden, lest it would be misunderstood by the forces of good and attacked with the same prejudice than those genuinely evil."\par
An undead minion: "Lurking in the darkness of fear of death, we have come to exact justice upon the wicked. How shall we do this, master?"\par
Silan: "Well, obviously we will have to hide from the elves. If everything goes well, we will be able to remove the orcs without them ever realizing we even were here."\par
Minion: "And if everything does not go well...?"\par
Silan: "..."\par
Silan: "Then we may be forced to defend ourselves from both sides of the conflict. Regardless of how it goes, we must try and keep the elvish casualties at the bare minimum."\par
\par
---\par
With the addition of skeletons and skeleton archers, Silan now has the full list of undead recruits in his possession.\par
All orcs concentrate entirely upon the elves, only turning on the undead when they're seen, and not going to actively look for the player.\par
There are some elves fighting in the northern front, but they are hopelessly outnumbered by the orcs. Most elves are in the west and southwest, where they are faring much better, but will also be eventually defeated and driven back to their city.\par
\par
The prime concern of this mission is elves and their attitude. They will start neutral and only attack the orcs, but if the player's forces are ever seen - i.e. any undead is at the vision range of an elf by the time the player's turn ends - they all turn hostile, go looking for the player, and have the following dialogue:\par
Elf: "An undead! The necromancers are aiding orcs against us?"\par
Silan: "Um, it's not actually like that. I am here to help y-"\par
Elf: "Cease your slithering tongue, villain! We will come deal with you now!"\par
Silan: "Augh. Why don't they ever listen?"\par
\par
If the elves do turn hostile, however the mission is still beatable by finishing off the orcs. The only way to be defeated is to either lose Silan or have the elvish chief killed, which will come with this bit of word:\par
Silan: "Oh, no. The elves have been routed."\par
An undead minion: "We have no more purpose here, master, do we?"\par
Silan: "No. I suppose we could defeat the remaining orcs, but the primary purpose of the act has been lost. We have been defeated here."\par
\par
---\par
On victory:\par
Minion: "Master, the orcs have been routed. Our task is finished."\par
Silan: "You are right, as usual! Let us withdraw!"\par
\par
If the player was seen during the mission:\par
Elf: "Look, sir. The mist is withdrawing. The undead are nowhere to be seen, either: we assume they conjured it in the first place, and it vanished along with them."\par
Elfish Marshal: "Cowards, fleeing as soon as we had finished with their orcish allies. They should have come here and met our blades!"\par
Elf: "But sir, there is something else. If you look at the scene, you see many, many dead orcs. Even without the undead, they should have been able to defeat us all with ease - let alone when they had necromancers as their allies."\par
\par
If the player wasn't seen, this dialogue is played instead:\par
Marshal: "The mist is finally clearing. I cannot help but wonder where it came from."\par
Elf: "Sir? We have discovered something interesting."\par
Marshal: "What of?"\par
Elf: "Well, first of all... there are many orcs on the battleground now. There was far more than any of our warriors could ever have slain - perhaps even enough to wipe us all out."\par
\par
Regardless of whether the player was seen or not, this will be said next:\par
Marshal: "Indeed, I can see what you mean. What else?"\par
Elf: "Sir, take a closer look at this body."\par
...\par
Marshal: "But... this is..."\par
Elf: "Indeed. It was not killed by an elfish weapon..."\par
Marshal: "...But by an orc."\par
Elf: "But why? Were they infighting? A power struggle, perhaps?"\par
Marshal: "No, I do not think so. I am not saying the orcs don't have such habits, but... take a look."\par
Elf: "Sir?"\par
Marshal: "The weapons that were used to slay this orc are old. Very, very old, and rusty."\par
Elf: "But... that might not mean anything. Orcs are not well known for their weapon maintenance, and often pass on swords and other equipment to their young, instead of making new ones."\par
Marshal: "...Yes. Whatever it was, an infighting or a mysterious ally, it most likely saved our lives. \par
Elf: "Intentionally or not."\par
Marshal: "The entire case is most peculiar."\par
\par
***********************************\par
\par
This mission would seem to have plenty of potential for branching campaign, depending on whether Silan was seen or not. But I'm not a talented enough writer to make such things without turning the whole thing into an overly complicated snarl.\par
