\par
Chapter Six: To Storm the Fortress atop the Mountains\par
\par
Weeks passed as they fought against the tide of the orcs, and the occasional undead. Despite being separated, Cheska, Silan, and Xel all came to more or less the same conclusions about the matter: the orcs were numerous and everywhere, but lacked any focus and concentration of any organized battleforce, instead raiding and killing very much randomly. This made it relatively easy to push them back as well.\par
\par
It was quite a mystery, however, what had gotten them on the move in the first place. Orcs have always been a warlike race, and launched frequent raids to the surrounding civilizations, but this was something else entirely.\par
\par
Winter had settled in and covered all of Wesnoth in snow, when Silan was at last reunited with the rest of the party. They each had all followed the tracks of the orc activity on their own, finally discovering the location of their warcamp: despite being relatively independent and working separately, there was likely to be a bigger, nastier orc behind it all pulling the strings.\par
\par
Together once more, the three plan on assaulting the orcs and ending this war once and for all...\par
\par
---\par
[Map]\par
(The topmost base belongs to Silan, while the bottom one is Cheska's. Bottom-right - in the water - is a bunch of nagas, while every other base is occupied by orcs. The camp walls surrounding the mountain have orcs as well: they mostly stay where they are, at least until something comes close enough and it's a pretty smart move for them to go elsewhere. Curiously enough, the one in charge of the whole thing - the leader of the central camp, in the middle of the mountain - is not an orc at all, but an Armageddon Drake . His lackeys are all orcs, however.)\par
\par
Silan: "Cheska! How wonderful to see you again! It has been all too long!"\par
Cheska: "...Likewise."\par
Silan: "You know, I was just watching for this place. I think it was very fortunate we met here: I probably could not have done this all alone."\par
Cheska: "Teaming up against a superior number of orcs for our own survival... Just like the old days, I suppose."\par
Silan: "Indeed, indeed! ...But where is Xel? I was hoping to meet him as well."\par
Cheska: "Xel took his saurians and a few sneaky humans, in an effort to sneak in to the base from the east. He is late, though: I hope nothing hasn't happened."\par
Silan: "Speaking of their base. Did you notice the...?"\par
Cheska: "I did."\par
\par
Orc grunt: "My lord, we are approached by a... strange combination of forces."\par
Lord Flameheart Galragoth: "Indeed? Would you care to elaborate this?"\par
Grunt: "Well... a necromancer with a bunch of undead from the north. And a human army, allied with a few drakes, from the south. We assume, my lord, that they are all here to bash our heads in."\par
Galragoth: "Yes, I believe this assumption might hold some truth in it. Prepare all forces for battle."\par
\par
Cheska: "A drake...? How in blazes can a drake be in charge of an orc army?"\par
Silan: "Beats me. I think it paid them money. A lot of money. Do you suppose it could be behind all this?"\par
Cheska: "Perhaps, although the motivation is unclear. What I know of drakes - most of it from Xel and his henchmen - is that they are quite happy with what they have, and don't usually run around conquering other people's places."\par
Silan: "Further supported by the fact that we indeed don't see many drakes running around in here trying to conquer our place. Suppose you would like to go and ask why he is doing this?"\par
\par
---\par
There are several orc bases, and one naga fort, in the area: all of them quickly recruit a bunch of troops and attack whoever is nearest. The drake's base, meanwhile, sits around without doing anything much until a hostile force approaches it.\par
Cheska can now recruit drakes, because they're too big and loud to go on Xel's sneaky secret mission. If Zeza is still alive, he is here as well, along with the other named units.\par
For this mission to be considered a success, all the player needs to do is to go and beat up Lord Flameheart Galragoth himself. Easier said than done: not only is he a bloody tough customer in his own right, but all his immediate minions are of second level. There are not many of them, though.\par
\par
On the tenth turn of conflict, Xel finally shows up. He appears to the mountains, straight east from the drake's base, along with him all the saurian veterans you have, as well as a few human rogues and outlaws. They're all in the player's control. Dialogue is triggered:\par
Xel: "Sorry I'm late! Did I miss anything?"\par
Cheska: "Xel! What kept you?"\par
Silan: "Great to see you again, little guy!"\par
Xel: "Hey, Silan is here! Nice to get back in touch with you, too."\par
\par
Galragoth: "What is this? Saurians? How did they get past our lines?"\par
Grunt: "I... don't know, my lord. Sneaky bunch, saurians, as I'm sure you already know. You want us to stop them?"\par
Galragoth: (Sarcastically) "If you would be so kind...?"\par
\par
Xel: "Wait, what? How can they have...?"\par
Silan: "Yes, we have been wondering the same thing. It's a... rather disturbing reveal, I think."\par
Cheska: "Whatever the case, we have more pressing matters to worry about right now. Onward, men!"\par
\par
Once anyone, after this point, actually manages to hurt the mighty drake, some more dialogue will follow:\par
Xel: "Oi! Would you mind telling us what you are up to?"\par
Galragoth: "I think not. You will warrant no villain's exposition from me."\par
\par
The battle is over when someone manages to injure Galragoth to less than twenty hit points. If the attack would have been enough to kill him, he's reduced to 1 HP instead.\par
\par
---\par
On victory:\par
Galragoth: "Enough!"\par
His next attack - I haven't decided if it should be a gust of wind, a mighty stomp on ground that causes earthquakes, or a ring of fire - hurts everyone right next to him, friend or foe, and pushes them back a few steps.\par
Galragoth: "Fine. So you all have enough battle prowess to storm past my orc lackeys and seriously hurt me. Perhaps you can be a threat to me after all. But I must go now: I have wasted too much time, and I shall not die by your hand, not here."\par
He spreads his wings.\par
Cheska: "Wait!"\par
Galragoth: "Till we meet again!"\par
\par
Then he flies away.\par
Silan: "Well, that was certainly... interesting."\par
Xel: "The rest of the orcs have been routed: whatever was left of them pretty much surrendered as soon as they saw their chief chickening out."\par
Cheska: "What took you so long, anyway? I thought something might have happened."\par
Xel: "Oh, um... nothing much. Just a few orcs. Infiltrating can be tough... he-heh. Nice of you to worry for me, though."\par
Cheska: "Oh it's nothing."\par
Silan: "Enough with the small-talk: I think the big question is, why was a drake in charge of them to begin with? Should we find him and ask?"\par
Cheska: "Probably explains why all the orcs were simultaneously so aggressive but also separate and uncoordinated, though: a member of another race in charge of them might be able to rouse and provoke them a bit, but not lead them all properly."\par
Xel: "I doubt it. Drakes of that magnitude hold a great deal of authority. He could easily have made them into an impressive and united battleforce if he wanted."\par
Silan: "Maybe he wasn't in charge after all?"\par
The reaction to this is amused, although Xel's laughter is extremely uncomfortable.\par
Cheska: "Ha-ha, yeah right."\par
Xel: "Ha-ha..."\par
...\par
Silan: "Xel, is something wrong?"\par
Xel: "...You are scaring me."\par
Silan: "How come?"\par
Xel: "Because you make sense. And because if something can command such a mighty drake..."\par
Cheska: "...then that something is a powerful force indeed."\par
\par
***********************************\par
\par
The plot thickens!\par
Also I'll give you some inside information: Galragoth (or his possible superiors) had to pay a LOT of money for the orcs, and then kill a few of them for good measure, for them to call him "Lord". Orcs really don't much like other races, so I've understood.\par
